<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap result">
		<div>
			<p class="eyebrow">What you get</p>
			<h2>Notes you can check, in plain text files.</h2>
			<p class="section-lead">Each note is a Markdown file in your Sub-Sub folder. Obsidian shows it well; any editor opens it.</p>
			<ul class="result-points">
				<li><strong>It says what it read.</strong> The full text, the abstract or only the metadata. Page numbers come only from a full text.</li>
				<li><strong>It cites only works it found.</strong> Library items by citekey; other works with their DOI or PMID.</li>
				<li><strong>You decide how much it writes.</strong> In the Reader profile, for students, a note is quotes and questions; you write the summary.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<figure class="note-card">
			<div class="note-bar"><span>Literature/smith2021.md</span><span>Reader</span></div>
			<div class="note-body">
<pre class="fm">citekey: smith2021
type: reading-note
evidence: full text</pre>
				<h3>Smith 2021, Home spirometry in children with asthma</h3>
				<blockquote><p>"Children did home spirometry twice a day for 12 weeks, with a parent, after one training session in the clinic." <span class="page">p. 3</span></p></blockquote>
				<p class="q">How did the authors check technique between clinic visits?</p>
				<blockquote><p>"Of 1,140 home manoeuvres, 71% met the ATS/ERS acceptability criteria." <span class="page">p. 5</span></p></blockquote>
				<p class="q">Would that rate hold in children under six?</p>
			</div>
		</figure>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="band">
	<div class="wrap split">
		<div>
			<p class="eyebrow">How a change happens</p>
			<h2>You see the change before it happens. You can undo it after.</h2>
			<ol class="steps">
				<li><strong>You ask in plain language.</strong> For example: tag the next 10 items without a topic.</li>
				<li><strong>Sub-Sub prepares the change as a dry run.</strong> The preview shows each item, with the tags it adds in green and the tags it removes in red.</li>
				<li><strong>You select Yes or No.</strong> The check is in the program, not in the instructions to the model. The model cannot skip it.</li>
				<li><strong>The change goes into a journal.</strong> <code>/undo</code> reverts it. Nothing is deleted: items go to the Zotero trash.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>
		<figure class="shot approval-shot">
			<img class="only-light" src="/assets/img/web-approval.webp?v={{ assetVersion }}" width="1280" height="640" loading="lazy" alt="An approval dialog. It asks: Apply tag items? Two items would change. One gets three new topic tags; the other gets one new tag and loses type/rct. Buttons: No and Yes.">
			<img class="only-dark" src="/assets/img/web-approval-dark.webp?v={{ assetVersion }}" width="1280" height="640" loading="lazy" alt="">
		</figure>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap split">
		<div>
			<p class="eyebrow">For students</p>
			<h2>It helps you read. It does not write your work.</h2>
		</div>
		<div class="prose-block">
			<p>Students start with the Reader profile: reading notes with quotes and questions, and a reading list for a literature review. No summaries or conclusions to hand in. The rules of your course about AI still apply.</p>
			<p>You can try it for free with a Google key, and an empty Zotero library is fine.</p>
			<p><a href="/students/">For students</a> · <a href="/pt/estudantes/" lang="pt-PT" hreflang="pt-PT">Para estudantes</a></p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap">
		<p class="eyebrow">Profiles</p>
		<h2>You decide how much it writes for you.</h2>
		<p class="section-lead">The mode (Researcher or Librarian) sets what Sub-Sub works on. The profile sets how much it writes for you, in either mode. Students start with Reader.</p>
		<div class="profiles">
			<div><h3>Reader</h3><p>For students. Explains each step. Reading notes are quotes with page numbers and a question for you under each quote. A literature search gives a reading list. No summaries.</p></div>
			<div><h3>Scholar</h3><p>The default. Literature notes, syntheses, literature reviews and all library changes. No text for your manuscripts.</p></div>
			<div><h3>Author</h3><p>Scholar, plus help with your manuscripts: citation checks, a bibliography, comments on the argument, reviews against reporting guidelines.</p></div>
			<div><h3>Editor</h3><p>Everything. When you ask, it drafts text for a manuscript and marks the drafted text so that you can check it.</p></div>
		</div>
		<p class="more"><a href="/profiles/">Compare the profiles</a> · <a href="/how-it-works/">How modes and profiles fit together</a></p>
	</div>
</section>

{%- if trust.items.length %}
<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap">
		<p class="eyebrow">In use</p>
		<h2>What people say.</h2>
		<div class="trust">
			{%- for q in trust.items %}
			<figure><blockquote>{{ q.quote }}</blockquote><figcaption>{{ q.who }}</figcaption></figure>
			{%- endfor %}
		</div>
		{%- if trust.facts.length %}
		<ul class="trust-facts">{% for f in trust.facts %}<li>{{ f }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
		{%- endif %}
	</div>
</section>
{%- endif %}

<section class="section">
	<div class="wrap split">
		<div>
			<p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
			<h2>Your library stays in Zotero.</h2>
		</div>
		<div class="prose-block">
			<p>Sub-Sub talks to Zotero through its local API and never uses your zotero.org account. It sends no usage data.</p>
			<p>The model provider that you choose receives your messages and what the tools return: titles, authors, abstracts, and parts of a full text when you ask for a note. PubMed, Europe PMC, OpenAlex, Crossref and Unpaywall receive search terms and identifiers, not your notes.</p>
			<p><a href="/privacy/">What goes where</a></p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

